"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The women’s high hurdles were expected to be a battle between Puerto Rican " [:a {:href "/athletes/146061"} "Jasmine Camacho-Quinn"] ", twice an NCAA Champion at the University of Kentucky, and American " [:a {:href "/athletes/147118"} "Keni Harrison"] ", the world record holder and the silver medallist at the 2019 World Championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the semi-finals Camacho-Quinn broke the Olympic record with her PR of 12.26. Harrison also qualified for the final, but was only second in the second semi behind Jamaican " [:a {:href "/athletes/144238"} "Britany Anderson"] ". Joining them in the final was the winner of the first semi-final, Nigerian " [:a {:href "/athletes/131229"} "Tobi Amusan"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The final turned into the expected dual between Camacho-Quinn and Harrison. Camacho-Quinn pulled ahead by hurdle six, and held on, despite clipping hurdle nine, and stutter-stepping hurdle 10, and won the gold medal, her 12.35 leading Harrison by 0.15 seconds. Behind them, Jamaica’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/130726"} "Meghan Tapper"] " won bronze just ahead of Amusan."] "\n\n" [:p {} "For Puerto Rico, this was only their second Olympic gold medal in any sport, after " [:a {:href "/athletes/131459"} "Monica Puig"] " won the women’s tennis singles in 2016. It was also the island’s second medal in athletics following a bronze medal in the 2012 men’s 400 hurdles by " [:a {:href "/athletes/114636"} "Javier Culson"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3248"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/131455"} "Jasmine Camacho-Quinn"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/PUR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/PUR.png"}] "PUR"]] [:td {} [:span {:clas